{"type": "init", "tier": "prototype", "idea": "Sign in with Google and carry every learner's progress, daily and weekly work across devices — instantly, offline-capable, with every sync visible on the page", "at": "2026-09-04T17:41:47.020Z"}
{"type": "note", "text": "the site is static on GitHub Pages: 103 pages generated by tools/build.py, zero network calls anywhere in the 4700-line app.js, and every piece of learner state in eleven localStorage keys under en8:. There is no service worker and no manifest. So 'learn everywhere' and 'instant' are both greenfield, and neither can be done by the page alone", "at": "2026-09-04T17:45:49.358Z"}
{"type": "note", "text": "Firebase Spark was chosen over Supabase for a reason that is not about features: Supabase pauses a free project after 7 days of inactivity, and this learner studies in bursts around a Saturday session. A paused project is a dark app until a human resumes it. Spark cannot bill at all — it has no payment method, so an exceeded quota returns RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED and resets at midnight Pacific rather than charging", "at": "2026-09-04T17:45:49.663Z"}
{"type": "note", "text": "Cloud Storage for Firebase has required the paid Blaze plan since 2026-02-03. It is the single trapdoor from free to billed, and it is why art and audio stay in the static build. That is also the better architecture independently: GitHub Pages is already a CDN, and the immutable/mutable split (art in the build, per-user progress in Firestore) is what any offline-capable app wants", "at": "2026-09-04T17:45:49.988Z"}
{"type": "note", "text": "the twelve 'recordings' are browser speechSynthesis, not audio files — zero bytes shipped. Shipped assets today total ~1.06MB against GitHub Pages' 1GB limit, so there is no size pressure that would justify moving assets to a backend", "at": "2026-09-04T17:45:50.328Z"}
{"type": "note", "text": "app.js states a rule over the reading log in its own words: it must never become a score, a band, a reading rate, a words-per-minute, a streak or a comparison with anyone. A dated daily/weekly work record is exactly where a streak counter would appear, so the rhythm gate scans the rendered page for one", "at": "2026-09-04T17:45:50.618Z"}
{"type": "note", "text": "no Java runtime existed on this machine; the Firestore emulator is a Java program (the Auth emulator is not). OpenJDK 26 was installed with the operator's approval. The harness resolves the keg-only path itself rather than editing a shell profile, so a fresh variant worktree needs no hand configuration", "at": "2026-09-04T17:45:50.816Z"}
